;;
;; REPL/RedTeam/[RT-A]_Economics.repl — FAMILY A: arithmetic & value.
;;
;; "Can the math be made to produce something other than what was intended?"
;;
;; The first attack in this file is not a rounding trick or an overflow. It is the cheaper kind:
;; the protocol prices one operation TWICE, in two places, and a client may choose which price to
;; pay. No guard is bypassed and no arithmetic is wrong — both routes do exactly what their code
;; says. The defect is that the code says two different things.
;;
;; Run: cd REPL && pact "RedTeam/[RT-A]_Economics.repl"
;;
(load "../deploy-stage00.repl")
(load "../deploy-stage01.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.2]_DPTF.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.3]_SWP.repl")

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "RT-A-001 - route around the lp-churn deterrent by picking the other Talos client")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-A-001 · 01 · the same operation, two Talos entrypoints, two prices ====
(print "--- [RT-A-001 · 01 · the same operation, two Talos entrypoints, two prices] ---")
;;<<RT-A-001>> FAMILY: A | STATUS: FIXED
;;HYPOTHESIS: adding standard liquidity is reachable through TWO live Talos client paths, and one
;;            of them does not charge the `lp-churn` deterrent. If so, the deterrent is optional:
;;            a liquidity provider pays it only if they pick the more expensive door.
;;METHOD:     read the flat leg each route charges. The defpact's step 0 bills NOTHING except its
;;            flat fee -- it quotes the pool, yields, and collects -- so the patron's IGNIS delta
;;            across the starting transaction isolates that leg exactly. The single-tx route's
;;            deterrent is read from the price table it bills through.
;;RESULT:     CONFIRMED, then FIXED. Measured exploit-first: the defpact door charged 53.0 (a flat
;;            literal 100.0 at this patron's 0.53 discount) where the single-tx door's deterrent is
;;            1051.0 raw / 557.03 net -- a 10.5x saving on the same operation on the same pool.
;;            Repaired in 20_MTX-SWP.pact; the assertions below now pin that both doors read one
;;            price table. The same gap was fixed in the Frozen and Sleeping twins at the same time.
;;
;;WHY THIS IS A REAL FINDING AND NOT A PRICING PREFERENCE. `lp-churn` is a DETERRENT: its purpose
;;is to make frequent add/remove cycling expensive, which protects the remaining liquidity providers
;;from having the pool churned around them. A deterrent that can be declined is not a deterrent. And
;;the cheaper door is the one the gas station subsidises.
;;
;;    TS01-C3::SWP|C_AddLiquidity          -> SWPLC   -> UC_IgnisPrice "..." "lp-churn"  = 1051.0
;;    TS01-CP::SWP|C_AddStandardLiquidity  -> MTX-SWP -> a literal                       =  100.0
;;
;;Both are `P|UEV_IMC`-gated Talos clients, so both are the supported path; this is not a matter of
;;calling core directly. `lp-churn` appears ZERO times in `20_MTX-SWP.pact`. The earlier repair that
;;put every add-liquidity op onto `UC_IgnisPrice` landed in `18_SWPLC.pact` only, and nothing
;;compared the two modules afterwards -- the suite measured each route against ITS OWN preview, and
;;both agreed with themselves.
;;
;;THAT IS THE GENERAL LESSON, and it is worth the paper: a preview-versus-charge check, however
;;rigorous, cannot see this class. It asks "does this route quote what it charges", and both routes
;;pass. The question nobody was asking is "do two routes to the same operation charge the same".
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] } { "key": "PK_Emma", "caps": [] }])
(let
    (
        (ref-TS01-CP:module{TalosStageOne_ClientPactsV4} TS01-CP)
        (ref-DALOS:module{OuronetDalosV2} DALOS)
        (ref-IGNIS:module{IgnisCollectorV2} IGNIS)
        (ref-SWPLC:module{SwapperLiquidityClientV2} SWPLC)
        (patron:string KST.ANHD)
        ;;the same standard pool modules/SWP.repl measures the SINGLE-TX route on at <<SWP-I14>>,
        ;;so the two figures are directly comparable rather than merely similar.
        (pool:string "S|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        (amounts:[decimal] [10.0 10.0 10.0])
    )
    (let
        (
            (discount:decimal (ref-DALOS::URC_IgnisGasDiscount patron))
            ;;the deterrent the OTHER Talos route bills, read from the table it bills through
            (single-tx-flat:decimal
                (ref-IGNIS::UC_IgnisPrice "SWP|C_AddStandardLiquidity" "lp-churn"))
            (stoa-pid:decimal (U|CT.UR_STOA-PID|Price))
        )
        (let
            (
                ;;READ BEFORE THE ADD -- the CLAD is a function of pool state, and the add moves it.
                (tax:decimal
                    (at "total-ignis-tax-needed"
                        (ref-SWPLC::URCi_AddStandardLiquidityClad patron pool amounts stoa-pid)))
                (quoted:decimal
                    (at "ignis-need" (at "ignis"
                        (INFO-ONE.INFO_SWP|AddStandardLiquidity patron patron pool amounts stoa-pid))))
                (ignis-before:decimal (ref-DALOS::UR_TF_AccountSupply patron false))
            )
            ;;DRIVE THE WHOLE DEFPACT INSIDE ONE TRANSACTION. `continue-pact` resolves against the
            ;;pact started in this same transaction, so all three steps -- and therefore the TOTAL
            ;;they bill -- can be bracketed by one pair of balance reads. Until 2026-09-14 this
            ;;block measured STEP 0 ALONE and compared it to a whole-operation figure, which is why
            ;;the assertions below used to be about the opening leg rather than the price of the op.
            ;;The idiom and its justification live in modules/DEFPACT-BILLING.repl.
            (ref-TS01-CP::SWP|C_AddStandardLiquidity patron patron pool amounts)
            (let
                (
                    (charged0:decimal (- ignis-before (ref-DALOS::UR_TF_AccountSupply patron false)))
                )
                (continue-pact 1)
                (continue-pact 2)
                (let
                    (
                        (total:decimal (- ignis-before (ref-DALOS::UR_TF_AccountSupply patron false)))
                    )
                    (map print
                        [
                            (format "<<RT-A-001>> defpact step-0={} | TOTAL={} | quoted={} + tax={} | deterrent={} raw @ discount={}"
                                [charged0 total quoted tax single-tx-flat discount])
                            ;;1] THE REPAIR, stated where a bypass would now have to hide: the TOTAL.
                            ;;   The multi-step door bills exactly what its preview quotes plus the
                            ;;   declared asymmetry tax -- the same identity modules/SWP.repl pins
                            ;;   for the SINGLE-TX door at <<SWP-I14>> (quoted + tax = real). Two
                            ;;   doors, one price. Before the repair the defpact door billed a flat
                            ;;   100.0 for the churn deterrent where the single-tx door billed
                            ;;   1051.0, and a liquidity provider could simply choose the cheap one.
                            ;;   This is an EQUALITY on a measured balance delta, so dropping any
                            ;;   leg -- including the deterrent remainder now collected in step 1 --
                            ;;   turns it red.
                            (expect "<<RT-A-001>> the defpact door bills exactly its quote plus the declared tax"
                                total (+ quoted tax))
                            ;;2] and it is the real TABLE price, not a second literal that happens to
                            ;;   match -- a literal would drift the moment the price table moved, which is
                            ;;   how the two doors came apart in the first place.
                            (expect "<<RT-A-001>> ...and that price is the table's: deter 1000 + this op's component 51"
                                1051.0 single-tx-flat)
                            ;;3] THE EXPLOIT, INVERTED, and MOVED TO THE TOTAL. This read TRUE before
                            ;;   the repair and must now be false. It is deliberately asserted on the
                            ;;   whole-pact total rather than on step 0, because step 0 is now SMALL
                            ;;   BY DESIGN -- the owner's 2026-09-14 ruling splits the fee so a pact
                            ;;   killed by an altered pool costs its owner only the initiation slice
                            ;;   (see RedTeam/[RT-F]_Griefing.repl <<RT-F-001>>). Asserting the old
                            ;;   "step 0 is not cheap" form would now fail on the FIX, and re-pointing
                            ;;   it at the total is what keeps it a bypass test instead of a
                            ;;   fee-schedule test.
                            (expect "<<RT-A-001>> ...so the ~10x discount the defpact door used to offer is GONE"
                                false (< total (* discount single-tx-flat)))
                            ;;4] the fee SPLIT itself, pinned: step 0 takes the initiation slice only.
                            ;;   This is the assertion that would catch the deterrent creeping back
                            ;;   into step 0 -- which is what RT-F-001 measured the cost of.
                            (expect "<<RT-A-001>> ...and step 0 takes only the initiation slice, not the deterrent"
                                (* discount 100.0) charged0)
                            ;;5] non-vacuity: the route really did DO something -- the pact ran to
                            ;;   completion and money moved, so this is a live add and not a call
                            ;;   that failed early.
                            (expect "<<RT-A-001>> ...and the route really executed, rather than failing early"
                                true (> total (* discount single-tx-flat)))
                        ]
                    )
                )
            )
        )
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(commit-tx)
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
;;|| NEXT                   >
;;||>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>
(begin-tx "RT-A-002 - the door that requires asymmetry is the one that does not tax it")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-A-002 · 01 · two doors, two prices ====
(print "--- [RT-A-002 · 01 · two doors, two prices] ---")
;;<<RT-A-002>> FAMILY: A | STATUS: REFUSED
;;HYPOTHESIS: this looks exactly like RT-A-001 from the outside, which is why it was chased.
;;            `MTX|C_AddLiquidity` takes `asymmetric-collection` and `gaseous-collection`, and the
;;            CORE picks them per door -- the client only picks the door:
;;                C_AddStandardLiquidity -> true  true    (asymmetry tax ON)
;;                C_AddIcedLiquidity     -> false true    (asymmetry tax OFF)
;;                C_AddGlacialLiquidity  -> false false   (neither tax)
;;            All three branches then call the IDENTICAL function with IDENTICAL arguments --
;;            `ref-SWPL::XE_STOA-PID|AddLiquidity account swpair asymmetric-collection
;;            gaseous-collection stoa-pid ld clad` -- differing only in which capability is
;;            acquired. And inside SWPL the flag gates nothing but TAX COLLECTION. So: same
;;            operation, two prices, caller picks. That is RT-A-001's shape exactly.
;;            Worse, `UEV_AddChilledLiquidity` enforces
;;            "Chilled Liquidity can only be added when asymtric liquidity exists" -- so THE TWO
;;            DOORS THAT REQUIRE ASYMMETRY ARE EXACTLY THE TWO THAT DO NOT TAX IT.
;;METHOD:     drive the SAME asymmetric input through both doors and compare the IGNIS charged, then
;;            look for what the cheaper door takes instead.
;;RESULT:     REFUSED. The tax is not skipped, it is EXCHANGED FOR A LOCK. Step 2 of the pact reads
;;                (if (not asymmetric-collection)
;;                    (ref-VST::C_Freeze SWP|SC_NAME account lp-id secondary) ...)
;;            -- so the doors that skip the asymmetry tax hand back FROZEN LP, locked in VST, while
;;            the Standard door hands back free LP. The provider pays in liquidity instead of IGNIS.
;;            A coherent product trade-off, not a bypass.
;;
;;            WHY THIS BLOCK EXISTS ANYWAY. The two consequences of one flag -- "no tax" and "LP is
;;            frozen" -- live FORTY LINES AND ONE DEFPACT STEP APART, in different capabilities. The
;;            price and the thing paid for it are not visible together anywhere. If a future change
;;            ever removed or conditioned that freeze, the bypass would become real and nothing
;;            would notice, because no test asserts the two are coupled. This one does.
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(let
    (
        (ref-SWPLC:module{SwapperLiquidityClientV2} SWPLC)
        (ref-SWP:module{SwapperV4} SWP)
        (patron:string KST.ANHD)
        (pool:string "S|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        (amounts:[decimal] [10.0 10.0 10.0])
    )
    (let
        (
            (stoa-pid:decimal (U|CT.UR_STOA-PID|Price))
        )
        (let
            (
                ;;the tax the STANDARD door quotes for this exact asymmetric input
                ;;untyped on purpose: the Clad row shape is not a named member of
                ;;SwapperLiquidityV2, so annotating it fails to load.
                (tax:decimal
                    (at "total-ignis-tax-needed"
                        (ref-SWPLC::URCi_AddStandardLiquidityClad patron pool amounts stoa-pid)))
            )
            (map print
                [
                    (format "<<RT-A-002>> standard-door asymmetry tax = {} | frozen-LP enabled on pool = {}"
                        [tax (ref-SWP::UR_IzFrozenLP pool)])
                    ;;1] the STANDARD door really does charge an asymmetry tax on this input --
                    ;;   without this the comparison below is meaningless.
                    (expect "<<RT-A-002>> the STANDARD door charges a real asymmetry tax here"
                        true (> tax 0.0))
                    ;;2] THE COUPLING, asserted from the other side: the cheaper doors are not
                    ;;   available at all unless the pool has FROZEN LP enabled -- which is what
                    ;;   makes the lock deliverable. A pool without it cannot use the untaxed door,
                    ;;   so "untaxed" and "locked" cannot come apart by pool configuration.
                    ;;   NOTE WHERE THE REFUSAL LIVES. Step 0 of the Iced door SUCCEEDS and collects
                    ;;   the initiation fee -- `UEV_AddChilledLiquidity` runs in STEP 1, so the
                    ;;   caller learns the door is unavailable only after paying. That is the
                    ;;   RT-F-001 shape, and it is now the documented design (owner ruling: 100 at
                    ;;   step 0, the rest at the succeeding step).
                    (expect "<<RT-A-002>> step 0 of the untaxed door charges before it validates"
                        true (!= "" (TS01-CP.SWP|C_AddIcedLiquidity patron patron pool amounts)))
                    (expect-failure "<<RT-A-002>> ...and STEP 1 refuses it: no frozen LP, no untaxed door"
                        "Frozen LP Functionality is not enabled on Swpair"
                        (continue-pact 1))
                ]
            )
        )
    )
    ""
)
(print "===^^^===")
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
